[INFO] 09-22 23:18:11 run_experiments.py:74] **Core Principle Identification**: The mean molecular speed in an ideal gas is derived from the Maxwell-Boltzmann distribution and requires the formula $ v_{\text{mean}} = \sqrt{\frac{8RT}{\pi M}} $, where $ R = 8.314 \, \text{J/(mol·K)} $ (universal gas constant), $ T $ is absolute temperature in Kelvin, and $ M $ is molar mass in **kg/mol**. Radon (Rn) has a molar mass of $ 222 \, \text{g/mol} $ (from its dominant isotope Rn-222), which must be converted to kg/mol for unit consistency with $ R $.
[INFO] 09-22 23:18:11 run_experiments.py:74] 
[INFO] 09-22 23:18:11 run_experiments.py:74] **Pitfall Prediction**:  
[INFO] 09-22 23:18:11 run_experiments.py:74] - *Conceptual Traps*: Confusing mean speed ($ \sqrt{8RT/(\pi M)} $) with root-mean-square speed ($ \sqrt{3RT/M} $) or most probable speed ($ \sqrt{2RT/M} $). Misinterpreting molar mass units (using g/mol instead of kg/mol, causing a $ \sqrt{1000} \approx 31.6\times $ error in the denominator).  
[INFO] 09-22 23:18:11 run_experiments.py:74] - *Calculation Traps*: Forgetting to convert $ 222 \, \text{g/mol} $ to $ 0.222 \, \text{kg/mol} $, leading to an incorrect speed value. Assuming room temperature ($ 298 \, \text{K} $) instead of standard temperature ($ 273 \, \text{K} $ for STP), which is conventional when unspecified.  
[INFO] 09-22 23:18:11 run_experiments.py:74] - *Completeness Traps*: Stopping after deriving the formula without computing the numerical value, or omitting unit verification (e.g., confirming $ \text{J} = \text{kg·m}^2/\text{s}^2 $ ensures $ v_{\text{mean}} $ is in m/s).
[INFO] 09-22 23:18:11 run_experiments.py:74] 
[INFO] 09-22 23:18:11 run_experiments.py:74] **Strategy Formulation**:  
[INFO] 09-22 23:18:11 run_experiments.py:74] 1. Convert radon’s molar mass from g/mol to kg/mol ($ 222 \, \text{g/mol} \rightarrow 0.222 \, \text{kg/mol} $).  
[INFO] 09-22 23:18:11 run_experiments.py:74] 2. Assume standard temperature $ T = 273 \, \text{K} $ (STP) since the problem does not specify temperature.  
[INFO] 09-22 23:18:11 run_experiments.py:74] 3. Substitute $ R = 8.314 \, \text{J/(mol·K)} $, $ T = 273 \, \text{K} $, and $ M = 0.222 \, \text{kg/mol} $ into $ v_{\text{mean}} = \sqrt{\frac{8RT}{\pi M}} $.  
[INFO] 09-22 23:18:11 run_experiments.py:74] 4. Compute the numerical value step-by-step, ensuring units cancel to m/s.  
[INFO] 09-22 23:18:11 run_experiments.py:74] 5. Verify the result aligns with expectations for a heavy gas (e.g., slower speed than lighter gases like oxygen).
[INFO] 09-22 23:18:11 run_experiments.py:74] </think>
